6	18	217	218	John Linden	4	" Uncle , you are not looking well to - night . "
19	38	217	218	John Linden	4	" I 'm not well , Florence . I sometimes doubt if I shall ever be any better . "
39	48	217	218	John Linden	4	" Surely , uncle , you can not mean---- "
49	67	217	218	John Linden	4	" Yes , my child , I have reason to believe that I am nearing the end . "
68	81	83	84	Florence Linden	2	" I can not bear to hear you speak so , uncle , "
90	105	83	84	Florence Linden	2	" You are not an old man . You are but fifty - four . "
106	152	217	218	John Linden	4	" True , Florence , but it is not years only that make a man old . Two great sorrows have embittered my life . First , the death of my dearly beloved wife , and next , the loss of my boy , Harvey . "
153	191	83	84	Florence Linden	2	" It is long since I have heard you refer to my cousin 's loss . I thought you had become reconciled -- no , I do not mean that,--I thought your regret might be less poignant . "
192	216	217	218	John Linden	4	" I have not permitted myself to speak of it , but I have never ceased to think of it day and night . "
225	321	217	218	John Linden	4	" If he had died , I might , as you say , have become reconciled ; but he was abducted at the age of four by a revengeful servant whom I had discharged from my employment . Heaven knows whether he is living or dead , but it is impressed upon my mind that he still lives , it may be in misery , it may be as a criminal , while I , his unhappy father , live on in luxury which I can not enjoy , with no one to care for me---- "
335	343	344	344	she	2	" Do n't say that , uncle , "
347	358	344	344	she	2	" You know that I love you , Uncle John . "
